kubeos-cli (1.0.0-7) trixie; urgency=medium

  * kubeos-node-join: take the kube-proxy identity from the cluster the node JUST JOINED,
    not from its dashboard kubeconfig. On a freshly installed machine that kubeconfig still
    points at the single-node cluster the installer brought up, and the join only re-points
    it at the very end - so the kube-proxy step authenticated against a server this node had
    already stopped: `config view` answered with the node's own address, every API call
    failed, and after 60 s of retries the join gave up. Measured 2026-11-20 on a fresh
    three-node cluster (installed from the packaged ISO): kubeos2 and kubeos3 joined, showed
    Ready, and had kube-proxy inactive, no /etc/kubernetes/kube-proxy.conf, no kube-proxy
    ServiceAccount, and the previous cluster's Service rules still in iptables DNATing
    cluster DNS to themselves - so no pod on either worker could resolve a name.
  * The endpoint now comes from the join string (or kubelet.conf, written by the join
    itself), and the pod-subnet fallback reuses the podCIDR step 4 already read, so neither
    value depends on a kubeconfig that may still describe the old cluster.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 00:30:00 +0530

kubeos-cli (1.0.0-6) trixie; urgency=medium

  * kubeos-node-join: read the API endpoint and the pod subnet with retries, and fall back
    to this node's own podCIDR, instead of giving up on the first empty answer. On kubeos2
    (2026-11-19) both reads came back empty while the API was still settling, the join logged
    one warning, and kube-proxy stayed unconfigured - which is not "no proxy": the unit kept
    the Service rules of the cluster the machine ran BEFORE the join, and those DNAT cluster
    DNS (10.96.0.10) to the worker itself, so every pod on the worker got "connection refused"
    from DNS while the control plane was fine.
  * The join now stops kube-proxy and flushes its stale KUBE-* chains before restarting it,
    and when it truly cannot configure the proxy it stops the unit instead of leaving the old
    rules in place.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Thu, 19 Nov 2026 22:55:00 +0530

kubeos-cli (1.0.0-1kubeos4) trixie; urgency=medium

  * kubeos-dashboard-install: decide "already installed" by the payload CHECKSUM, not by
    RELEASE. Every kubeos-dashboard build of a version carries the same release directory
    name, so `apt install --reinstall kubeos-dashboard` on a live node did nothing at all -
    the old unpacked tree and its plugin directories stayed in place. Found when a rebuilt
    dashboard package shipped a plugin under a different id and the node kept serving the
    old one (measured 2026-11-19).
  * It also prunes plugin directories the new release no longer carries, because the unpack
    merges into the existing payload directory instead of replacing it.

kubeos-cli (1.0.0-1kubeos3) trixie; urgency=medium

  * dh_installsystemd gets --no-enable only, not --no-start. --no-start also stops the
    units during an upgrade and never starts them again: upgrading this package took the
    whole control plane down (all eight units inactive, kubectl refused on :6443), measured
    on the live node on 2026-11-19. --no-enable alone is what was needed - it stops the
    package from enabling units (flanneld above all), while the normal stop/start around a
    binary upgrade keeps the cluster running.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:40:00 +0530

kubeos-cli (1.0.0-1kubeos2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Stop dh_installsystemd from enabling and starting/restarting units by itself. It
    were enabling flanneld.service - which hook 9050 documents as never-to-enable, since
    it cannot run as a host service - and it restarted units on every upgrade, including
    control-plane units that must not start before node-setup has written the PKI. The
    .wants links shipped in the package now decide, and nothing else.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:20:00 +0530

kubeos-cli (1.0.0-1kubeos1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Initial packaging. The scripts and the systemd units are the ones the KubeOS
    ISO shipped in config/includes.chroot, unchanged; the units move from
    /etc/systemd/system to /usr/lib/systemd/system because they are package-owned
    now, and the .wants links are shipped as files instead of being created by a
    build hook (systemctl enable in a postinst aborts a chroot build).

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Thu, 19 Nov 2026 09:40:00 +0530
